ght\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2901em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3531em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{i \\mathcal{C}_c} \\, \\nu_{g_{l \\in \\partial c}, m_{p \\in \\partial c}, \\widehat{n}_{p \\in \\partial c}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1944em;vertical-align:-0.3531em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathcal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1645em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:-0.0583em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">c</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.143em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0637em;\">ν</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3194em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0637em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.3488em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0197em;\">l</span><span class=\"mrel mtight\">∈</span><span class=\"mord mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">c</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1792em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">m</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.3488em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span><span class=\"mrel mtight\">∈</span><span class=\"mord mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">c</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2901em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord accent mtight\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6706em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"top:-3.1306em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"mtight\" style=\"height:0.24em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"100%\" height=\"0.24em\" viewBox=\"0 0 1062 239\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><path d=\"M529 0h5l519 115c5 1 9 5 9 10 0 1-1 2-1 3l-4 22\nc-1 5-5 9-11 9h-2L532 67 19 159h-2c-5 0-9-4-11-9l-5-22c-1-6 2-12 8-13z\"/></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.3488em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span><span class=\"mrel mtight\">∈</span><span class=\"mord mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">c</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2901em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3531em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, respectively. Also, I am missing discussion of the consistency requirement that these new partition functions locally reduce to the desired ordinary ones.</p>\n<p>For the moment I give up on following this. But if anyone has more insights to offer, I’d be interested.</p>","timestamp":1724843602,"subject":"topological charges in lattice gauge theory via group stacks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":118923,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/topological.20charges.20in.20lattice.20gauge.20theory.20via.20group.20stacks/near/118923\">#4</a> Meanwhile there was a followup:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peng Zhang, Jing-Yuan Chen: <em>An Explicit Categorical Construction of Instanton Density in Lattice Yang-Mills Theory</em> [<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.07195\">arXiv:2411.07195</a>]</li>\n</ul>","timestamp":1739249453,"subject":"topological charges in lattice gauge theory via group stacks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":124292,"sender_id":1515,"sender_full_name":"Yerden","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/389f3a1d57ab2c5abd46d99b237669ad?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/topological.20charges.20in.20lattice.20gauge.20theory.20via.20group.20stacks/near/124292\">#5</a> I am not sure as I didn't get appropriate training on the theme, but isn't one or several of nLab entries states that \"computer models predict around 1 instated per femtometer\", which suggests that computers can model instatons, or is it just misunderstanding and models mean our calculation that don' use lattice methods?</p>","timestamp":1775469399,"subject":"topological charges in lattice gauge theory via group stacks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":124293,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/topological.20charges.20in.20lattice.20gauge.20theory.20via.20group.20stacks/near/124293\">#6</a> You are probably thinking of the “instanton liquid model” briefly mentioned <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/instanton%20in%20QCD#Idea\">here</a> at <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/instanton%20in%20QCD\">instanton in QCD</a></em>.</p>\n<p>This is “confirmed” by lattice computations according to <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/instanton+in+QCD#AthenodorouEtAl2018\">Athenodorou et al. 2018</a>, but only up to significant handwaving, I think.</p>\n<p>Here is how <code>Gemini</code> describes the situation:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The findings of Athenodorou et al. serve as a practical illustration of the theoretical problem Jing-Yuan Chen is highlighting. The methods used by Athenodorou et al.—while practically useful—lack a mathematically rigorous and fundamental definition of what an instanton actually is on a discretized lattice.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>A natural definition for the instanton density operator on the lattice has been missing, and that traditional methods fail to capture the continuous homotopy information of the continuum. Athenodorou et al. demonstrate this failure explicitly. Because they lack a rigorous local operator for the raw lattice, they are forced to use the “Green’s functions” method to infer the raw instanton density of  <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.3</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12.3 \\mathrm{fm}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12.3</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">fm</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.1473em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. However, this method is purely indirect. It works by looking at the momentum dependence of the strong coupling and fitting it to an analytical, phenomenological model (the Instanton Liquid Model Ansatz). Chen would point out that this is not a measurement of a topological charge; it is an inference based on a model assumption. Chen’s goal is to construct an explicit categorical operator that can measure the instanton density directly on the raw lattice without needing a model-dependent fit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>To actually “see” the instantons locally, Athenodorou et al. are forced to use gradient flow to strip away ultraviolet fluctuations. However, as they themselves note, this smoothing process destroys instanton/anti-instanton pairs, drastically altering the physical properties of the vacuum and artificially driving the density down from  <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim12 \\mathrm{fm}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∼</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">fm</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.1473em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> to  <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim 1 \\mathrm{fm}^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∼</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">fm</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.1473em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. Chen identifies this reliance on smoothing—and the ambiguities it introduces—as a fundamental flaw in standard lattice gauge theory. From Chen’s mathematical standpoint, an exact topological operator should not require you to artificially alter the gauge field configuration (which destroys short-distance topological information) just to extract an integer charge.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>","timestamp":1775473740,"subject":"topological charges in lattice gauge theory via group stacks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]